<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened Runtime entitlements for the Developer ID build.

  Hardened Runtime is mandatory for notarization, and it switches OFF several
  things Chromium needs by default. Each entitlement below is here because
  without it the app crashes or a feature dies, not for good measure:

    allow-jit + allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at runtime. Hardened Runtime
      forbids that outright, so the app fails to launch at all.

    disable-library-validation
      The vendored libraries (pdf.js, pdf-lib, the Tesseract WASM runtime)
      ship unpacked outside the asar, and Electron loads its own framework
      dylibs. Library validation requires every loaded library to be signed
      by the same Team ID, which these are not.

    device.audio-input
      Volt's read-aloud and dictation features use the microphone. Without
      this the OS denies the device and the feature silently does nothing.
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist (set via mac.extendInfo)
      supplies the sentence macOS shows when it asks the user.

  Deliberately NOT here: app-sandbox. Volt opens and writes PDFs anywhere the
  user points it, watches files on disk for external edits, and talks to a
  local Ollama server on localhost. Sandboxing all of that is a Mac App Store
  problem, and the App Store cannot ship an app that updates itself - which
  is the whole point of the release feed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
